# InfantGod Studio language descriptor
# Yarn Spinner dialogue -- the format 《幼神》 is written in.
#
# Colouring targets, in the order the tokenizer scans them:
#   block comment /* */  ->  line comment //  ->  string "..."  ->
#   number  ->  word  ->  literal  ->  punctuation
#
# Only the 8 frozen keys are used; every choice below is forced by that grammar.
#
# 1. `line_comment //` is safe for this dialect: Yarn writes a command as `<<...>>`
#    and a node separator as `->`, so there is no legitimate `//` in the parts of a
#    .yarn file we colour.  URLs in prose would be a false positive; 《幼神》's prose
#    is Chinese and carries none.
# 2. `block_comment_open /*` / `block_comment_close */` cannot be written at all in
#    Yarn.  They are declared anyway because the brief asks for them and because an
#    author pasting a commented-out snippet gets a sane colour instead of a wall of
#    punct.  Comments outrank strings, so a `/*` inside a quoted title would win --
#    an accepted grammar limitation, not a choice.
# 3. `string "\""` matches Yarn's own requirement that `title:` and `when:` values
#    and every `<<if>>` operand be double-quoted.
# 4. Word kinds.  `kind keyword` covers the three positions where a bare English word
#    carries meaning: header keys, builtin commands, and boolean literals.  `true
#    false null` belong to `keyword` rather than `constant` because the grammar has no
#    notion of scope and a keyword colour is what an author expects to see there.
#    Giving them their own `constant` line would colour identically only if the theme
#    happened to agree -- the brief picks one, so they live with the keywords.
# 5. Literal kinds.  All of them are `command`: they are the ceremony of the format
#    -- node headers, node separators, the end marker, expressions, hashtags, the jump
#    arrow and grouping punctuation.  `->` and `-` are deliberately both declared;
#    longest-first matching is what makes `->` win, and that is the same mechanism
#    that makes `<<` win over `<`.
# 6. `<<` and `>>` are declared alongside `<` and `>`.  Yarn has no bare `<`, so a
#    stray one is prose, but declaring both keeps the longest-first rule load-bearing
#    and testable (`langs/selftest.mjs` asserts the `<<` span is one span, not two).
# 7. No `number` tokens are declared.  The tokenizer produces `number` from digits on
#    its own, which is what colours `#line:0630975` and `$Fan_Join` operands; a
#    declared token would add nothing and could only add false positives.
#
# Known, accepted limitations (properties of the frozen grammar, not fixable here):
#   * A declared keyword also colours inside prose, so an English word like `if` in a
#     line of dialogue becomes a keyword.  《幼神》's prose is Chinese, so this is rare.
#   * `$variable` is a literal `$` followed by an `ident`; the grammar cannot say
#     "dollar followed by an identifier is one token".  The two spans sit adjacent, so
#     the colour is still correct at the boundary.
id yarn
title Yarn
ext .yarn
ext .yarnproject
line_comment //
block_comment_open /*
block_comment_close */
string "\""
kind keyword "title" "when" "tags" "subtitle" "tracking" "position"
kind keyword "if" "elseif" "else" "endif" "set" "declare" "jump" "detour" "return" "stop" "once" "endonce" "wait"
kind keyword "true" "false" "null"
kind command "<<" ">>" "---" "===" "->" "#" "$" ":" "," "(" ")" "[" "]" "{" "}" "|"
kind command "<" ">"
